test('saves the profile', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/settings/profile');
  await page.getByLabel('Display name').fill('Ada');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).click();

  await page.waitForTimeout(2000);
  expect(await page.getByRole('status').textContent()).toBe('Profile saved');
});

referenceAnswer.en:
Drop the sleep and use the auto-retrying web-first assertion, which re-resolves the locator and re-checks the condition until the expect timeout:

```ts
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).click();
await expect(page.getByRole('status')).toHaveText('Profile saved');
```

`expect(await locator.textContent())` resolves the text once and then compares a plain string, so it cannot recover from a slow response. `page.waitForTimeout` is discouraged outside debugging for exactly this reason: it hard-codes a guess about latency that a loaded CI runner will eventually violate.

referenceAnswer.ru:
